        "math_academy_id": "q-114926",
        "knowledge_point_id": "2fca8969-c914-5ed2-a492-0addc197ce72",
        "knowledge_point": "Calculating Inverse Trigonometric Function Integrals Using Substitution",
        "problem": "$\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse secant:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(2x-7).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution \n\n$$\nu=2x-7.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|2x-7|\\sqrt{(2x-7)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =2\\,\\text{arcsec}(2x-7)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
