        "math_academy_id": "q-114910",
        "knowledge_point_id": "6aa497df-853e-5e77-9d43-6183fa53ffff",
        "knowledge_point": "Computing an Arctangent Integral by Completing the Square",
        "problem": "$\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+18 & ={x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+({3}^{2}-{3}^{2})+18 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2})-9+18 \\\\  & =(x-3)^{2}+9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x-3)^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u$ as the variable, we have $(\\frac{x-3}{3}).$\n\nSo, let's perform the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{x-3}{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-6x+18}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+((x-3)/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
